From a2775b2545fb72148e3c7ea67491a3ab70aff42f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mark-vis <118430440+mark-vis@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 05:53:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/14] Remove the usual separator after the label of \caption*

The label \@caption@nolabel of \caption* still typesets nothing, but
while \if@captionstar is true it now also removes the usual separators
after it: a colon or a period directly after the label (spaces before
it are skipped), then spaces, ~, \nobreakspace, \space, \ , \enspace,
\enskip, \quad and \qquad, also after the end of a group or of \textbf
(|{#1}: #2|, |\textbf{#1}: #2|). So the standard classes, many classes
derived from them and classes such as llncs, IEEEtran and mwart give
"Text" instead of ": Text" without any change. If something else follows
in the group of the label (a separator in a macro as in KOMA-Script,
memoir, amsart or babel-french, other space, a character), it is
printed before the text and a warning is given once per document. The
paragraph is started where removed material would have started it, and
\unskip, \unkern and \unpenalty after the label are removed as well.
The cases that are printed without a warning are listed in ltfloat.

This goes beyond the contract (\if@captionstar true, a label that
typesets nothing), which classes should test, but leaving it out is not
free. Without it, every class that does not test the switch prints its
separator before the text: the standard classes article, report and
book (classes.dtx does not test it) give ": Text" for \caption*, while
the same document with \DocumentMetadata{tagging=on} gives "Text"
(latex-lab's \@makecaption tests the switch); llncs, IEEEtran and mwart
give their separator as well, and the float first aid (the next commit)
relies on it for float's plain style ({\@fs@cfont #1:} #2).

The alternative is that classes.dtx tests \if@captionstar. That was
the first version of this proposal and was withdrawn: babel-french
installs its French separator only if \@makecaption is the standard
definition (\STD@makecaption), and caption3 recognises the standard
classes by the same comparison, so any change to the standard
\@makecaption gives "Figure 1: text" instead of "Figure 1 -- text" in
French pdfLaTeX documents (caption's test/babel/french-0, -4, -5), and
caption3 then reports an unknown class and uses its defaults. Changing
classes.dtx would need babel-french and caption to follow.

New test caption-interface-005 (the shapes of common \@makecaption
definitions, horizontal and vertical mode, warnings; the article
\@makecaption gives "Text" without a warning).
caption-interface-004 shows the new meaning of \fnum@<type>.
